steps.reasoning:
Reconstruct the per-round u-Ticket sequence (algorithmic core) as implemented by the authors, reproducing the procedural steps that modify the LTH loop: for pruning round index $i$,
- train the network $f(x;\theta \odot m_i)$ to convergence (here $m_i$ is the connectivity mask at round $i$);
- obtain the magnitude-pruned binary mask $\hat{m}_i$ by pruning low-magnitude weights from the trained network $f(x;\theta_{trained}\odot m_i)$;
- for each layer $l$, compute the PE workload list $W^{l}$ (counts of non-zero weights assigned to each of the $n$ PEs for that layer) and compute $w_{avg}^{l}=\mathrm{GetAverage}(W^{l})$;
- for each PE with current workload $w$ in $W^{l}$, if $w < w_{avg}^{l}$ then randomly recover (i.e., set to 1 in the mask) exactly $(w_{avg}^{l}-w)$ previously-zeroed weight connections in that PE's partition of layer $l$; otherwise if $w > w_{avg}^{l}$ then randomly remove (i.e., set to 0 in the mask) exactly $(w-w_{avg}^{l})$ non-zero connections from that PE's partition of layer $l$;
- after adjusting every layer's mask entries so each PE has workload equal to $w_{avg}^{l}$, reinitialize the surviving weights to the original initialization and proceed to the next pruning round.
This sequence exactly mirrors the pseudocode the authors presented (their iterative LTH loop augmented by the per-layer, per-PE RandomlyRecover / RandomlyRemove adjustments).
